SUMMARY:Deadline 17:00 CET: European Cultural Foundation: ERASMUS 40 Creati
 ve Research Fellowship (Florence archives\, Brussels presentation)
DESCRIPTION:Closes 01 Dec 2026 at 17:00 CET.\n\nTo mark the 40th anniversar
 y of ERASMUS in 2027\, the European Cultural Foundation is opening its his
 torical archives for a Creative Research Fellowship: one professional crea
 tive practitioner will research ECF's lesser-known role in shaping the ERA
 SMUS student mobility programme (from the European Institute of Education 
 and the Office for Cooperation in Education to the ERASMUS Bureau\, which 
 ran...\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural Foundation (ECF)\, with the Hist
 orical Archives of the European Union (EUI) and the House of European Hist
 ory\nLocation: Research stay in Florence\, Italy (Historical Archives of t
 he EU) in the first trimester of 2027\; final work presented in Brussels i
 n September 2027. Open to creative practitioners residing in a Council of 
 Europe member state.\nAward: EUR 10\,000 grant covering travel\, living ex
 penses and artistic production. One fellowship.\nDuration: Research reside
 ncy in Florence in the first trimester of 2027 (January to March)\; presen
 tation in September 2027\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Research stay in Florence\, Italy (Historical Archives of the EU) 
 in the first trimester of 2027\; final work presented in Brussels in Septe
 mber 2027. Open to creative practitioners residing in a Council of Europe 
 member state.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surf-artist-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sanford Underground Research Facility: Artist-in-Residenc
 e Program 2027 (Lead\, South Dakota)
DESCRIPTION:The SURF Artist-in-Residence program invites artists to create 
 work inspired by the Sanford Underground Research Facility in Lead\, South
  Dakota. At 4\,850 feet deep\, SURF is the deepest underground laboratory 
 in the United States\, housed in the former Homestake Gold Mine\, and host
 s experiments in physics\, biology\, geology and engineering.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Sanford Underground Research Facility (SURF)\nLocation: Sanford Under
 ground Research Facility\, Lead\, South Dakota\, USA\, in the Black Hills.
  The call does not state a residency or nationality restriction\; travel\,
  housing and per diem for both visits are covered.\nAward: USD 5\,000 stip
 end paid in two installments\, plus up to USD 5\,000 in additional funds t
 he artist may apply for to create the artwork. Transportation (airfare\, r
 ental car or mileage)\, housing and per diem are covered for both visits\;
  a studio space and guided surface and underground tours are provided\; re
 turn shipping of the exhibition is covered up to USD 7\,000.\nDuration: Fo
 ur-week on-site residency in summer 2027 (scheduled between 22 May and 31 
 July in coordination with SURF)\, plus a one-week exhibition installation 
 and guest-lecture visit in October 2027.\n\nMore: https://sanfordlab.org/p
 rograms/SURFAiR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sanfordlab.org/programs/SURFAiR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sanford Underground Research Facility\, Lead\, South Dakota\, USA\
 , in the Black Hills. The call does not state a residency or nationality r
 estriction\; travel\, housing and per diem for both visits are covered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khk-cure-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261130
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261201
SUMMARY:Deadline: KHK CURE Fellowships 2027/2028\, Saarland University (the
 me: Things)
DESCRIPTION:The Kaete Hamburger Centre for Cultural Practices of Reparation
  (KHK CURE) at Saarland University invites applications for up to twelve f
 ellowships for its fourth year\, October 2027 to September 2028.\n\nOrgani
 sation: Kaete Hamburger Centre for Cultural Practices of Reparation (CURE)
 \, Saarland University\nLocation: Saarbruecken\, Germany. Fellows must liv
 e in Saarbruecken during the fellowship and participate actively in centre
  events\; extended research absences are not permitted. Open international
 ly\; retired persons and Saarland University staff are not eligible.\nAwar
 d: Up to twelve fellowships. Fellows on unpaid leave from their institutio
 n receive approximately EUR 6\,100 to 7\,200 per month before taxes\, depe
 nding on qualifications\; alternatively\, fellows retaining their salary c
 an have their home institution reimbursed for replacement teaching. One ro
 und trip to Saarbruecken is reimbursed\, a fully equipped individual works
 pace is provided\, and an apartment can be arranged for around EUR 1\,250 
 per month. Eligible accompanying family members may receive additional sup
 port. No application fee.\nDuration: Six to twelve months\, beginning 1 Oc
 tober 2027\n\nMore: https://cure.uni-saarland.de/en/fellows/call-for-appli
 cations?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cure.uni-saarland.de/en/fellows/call-for-applications?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Saarbruecken\, Germany. Fellows must live in Saarbruecken during t
 he fellowship and participate actively in centre events\; extended researc
 h absences are not permitted. Open internationally\; retired persons and S
 aarland University staff are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucla-meap-modern-endangered-archives-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART:20261117T072900Z
DTEND:20261117T075900Z
SUMMARY:Deadline 23:59 PST: UCLA Modern Endangered Archives Program (MEAP):
  2026-27 Call for Applications
DESCRIPTION:Closes 16 Nov 2026 at 23:59 PST.\n\nThe UCLA Library Modern End
 angered Archives Program (MEAP)\, funded by Arcadia\, grants its ninth coh
 ort of awards to preserve\, document and digitize cultural heritage collec
 tions at risk from environmental conditions\, political uncertainty\, inhe
 rently unsustainable media\, inappropriate storage\, or communal and socia
 l change.\n\nOrganisation: UCLA Library\, Modern Endangered Archives Progr
 am\nLocation: Worldwide. Projects digitize at-risk collections where they 
 are held\; digitized materials are published open access in the UCLA Libra
 ry Digital Collections.\nAward: Planning Grants up to USD 20\,000 (up to 1
  year) for collection evaluation\, survey and inventory work before digiti
 zation. Project Grants up to USD 70\,000 (up to 2 years) for digitization 
 and metadata creation on well-organized collections\, or curation of exist
 ing digital assets. Regional Grants up to USD 100\,000 (up to 2 years) for
  digital collections drawing on three or more institutions\, families or r
 epositories around one theme\, hosted by an organization with prior MEAP e
 xperience. A separate AV Grants track (up to USD 100\,000\, for audio\, vi
 deo and film) opens in April 2027. No application fee.\nDuration: Planning
  Grants up to 1 year\; Project and Regional Grants up to 2 years\n\nMore: 
 https://meap.library.ucla.edu/call-for-applications/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://meap.library.ucla.edu/call-for-applications/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide. Projects digitize at-risk collections where they are he
 ld\; digitized materials are published open access in the UCLA Library Dig
 ital Collections.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
