UID:aaas-stpf-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Science & Technology Policy Fellowships\, 2027-28 Class
DESCRIPTION:The AAAS Science & Technology Policy Fellowships place scientis
 ts and engineers inside US federal agencies\, congressional offices and th
 e judicial branch for a year of hands-on policymaking\, contributing techn
 ical knowledge and analytical skills to the federal policy process.\n\nOrg
 anisation: American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)\nLoc
 ation: Washington DC\, USA. Fellows are placed in US federal executive\, l
 egislative and judicial branch offices and must reside in the DC area.\nAw
 ard: Stipend depending on experience and placement\, roughly USD 85\,000 t
 o 140\,000\; the dedicated AI legislative branch cohort is paid at GS-12 S
 tep 10 on the DC locality scale. Includes health insurance\, a travel and 
 professional development allowance\, and relocation support for some place
 ments. No application fee.\nDuration: One year\, 1 September 2027 to 31 Au
 gust 2028\, renewable in some placements\n\nMore: https://www.aaas.org/pro
 grams/science-technology-policy-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.aaas.org/programs/science-technology-policy-fellowships?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA. Fellows are placed in US federal executive\, 
 legislative and judicial branch offices and must reside in the DC area.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:acls-digital-justice-grants-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261104
SUMMARY:Deadline: ACLS Digital Justice Grants 2026-27 (Seed and Development
 )
DESCRIPTION:ACLS Digital Justice Grants fund digital projects in the humani
 ties and interpretive social sciences that critically engage with the inte
 rests and histories of people of colour and other historically marginalise
 d communities\, including Black\, Latinx\, Indigenous\, disabled and LGBTQ
 + communities.\n\nOrganisation: American Council of Learned Societies (ACL
 S)\, funded by the Mellon Foundation\nLocation: Grant funds must be admini
 stered by a US institution of higher education. No citizenship or residenc
 y restriction is stated for the principal investigator.\nAward: Seed Grant
 s USD 10\,000 to 25\,000 for start-up and prototyping work\; Development G
 rants USD 50\,000 to 100\,000 for projects past proof of concept. Institut
 ional indirect costs are not covered. No application fee.\nDuration: Grant
  terms run 12 to 18 months\, starting between 1 July 2027 and 31 December 
 2027. Deadline 3 November 2026\, 17:00 ET.\n\nMore: https://www.acls.org/p
 rograms/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.acls.org/programs/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Grant funds must be administered by a US institution of higher edu
 cation. No citizenship or residency restriction is stated for the principa
 l investigator.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asf-fellowships-americans-nordic-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: American-Scandinavian Foundation: Fellowships for America
 ns in the Nordic Countries 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:The American-Scandinavian Foundation's award program for study 
 and research abroad is its longest-standing commitment to educational exch
 ange: over the past 100 years\, more than 4\,000 fellowships and grants ha
 ve gone to Americans working in the Nordic region and Scandinavians in the
  US engaged in study\, research\, or creative arts projects.\n\nOrganisati
 on: American-Scandinavian Foundation\nLocation: Nordic region (Denmark\, F
 aroe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Finland\, Norway\, Sweden\, and Sapmi
 ). For American graduate students\, academic professionals\, and creative 
 artists\; project must take place in the Nordic countries.\nAward: Long-te
 rm fellowships of up to USD 23\,000 for 4 to 12 months\; short-term grants
  of up to USD 5\,000 for 1 to 3 months. In 2026\, 31 American Fellows rece
 ived over USD 277\,390 in total funding. Awards are given in all fields\, 
 including creative arts projects.\nDuration: 1 to 3 months (grants) or 4 t
 o 12 months (fellowships)\; year-long fellows submit an interim report\, a
 nd all fellows submit a final narrative report within 60 days of the fello
 wship's end.\n\nMore: https://www.amscan.org/fellowships-grants/grants-and
 -awards-for-americans/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://www.amscan.org/fellowships-grants/grants-and-awards-for-america
 ns/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nordic region (Denmark\, Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Fin
 land\, Norway\, Sweden\, and Sapmi). For American graduate students\, acad
 emic professionals\, and creative artists\; project must take place in the
  Nordic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lawai-summer-fellowship-us-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270129
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270130
SUMMARY:Deadline: Institute for Law & AI: Summer Fellowship US 2027 (Washin
 gton DC)
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for Law & AI's US Summer Fellowship is a paid res
 earch fellowship at the leading edge of US AI law and policy for law stude
 nts\, professionals\, and academics\, including the Summer Institute on La
 w and AI in Washington DC and mentorship from LawAI research staff.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Institute for Law & AI (LawAI)\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, USA
  (relocation expected\; USD 10\,000 relocation/housing stipend for DC-base
 d fellows). Requires valid US work authorization\; visa sponsorship availa
 ble but not guaranteed.\nAward: USD 2\,000 per week subsidy\, plus a USD 1
 0\,000 relocation/housing stipend for DC-based fellows and covered travel\
 , accommodation and meals for the Convening Week\; higher subsidies possib
 le for exceptional candidates. No application fee.\nDuration: 14 June to 2
 0 August 2027\, full-time\; Convening Week (Summer Institute on Law and AI
 ) 21-25 June in Washington DC.\n\nMore: https://law-ai.org/summer-fellowsh
 ip-us/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://law-ai.org/summer-fellowship-us/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, USA (relocation expected\; USD 10\,000 relocatio
 n/housing stipend for DC-based fellows). Requires valid US work authorizat
 ion\; visa sponsorship available but not guaranteed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:harry-ransom-center-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261106
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261107
SUMMARY:Deadline: Harry Ransom Center Research Fellowships 2027-2028 (Austi
 n\, Texas)
DESCRIPTION:The Harry Ransom Center at The University of Texas at Austin aw
 ards research fellowships for on-site work in its collections spanning lit
 erature\, photography\, film\, art\, performing arts\, music and cultural 
 history\, supporting scholarly articles\, dissertations\, creative works\,
  digital humanities projects and exhibition proposals.\n\nOrganisation: Ha
 rry Ransom Center\, The University of Texas at Austin\nLocation: Austin\, 
 Texas\, USA. On-site research in the Ransom Center's collections\; interna
 tional applicants welcome and receive an additional one-time USD 500 stipe
 nd for visa and travel costs.\nAward: Postdoctoral Fellowships: USD 3\,500
  per month for one or two months. Dissertation Fellowships: USD 2\,500. Tr
 avel Stipends: USD 2\,000 (open to graduate students\, faculty and indepen
 dent researchers including artists and journalists). International recipie
 nts get an extra USD 500. No application fee.\nDuration: One to two months
  on-site\, taken between 1 June 2027 and 31 August 2028.\n\nMore: https://
 www.hrc.utexas.edu/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://www.hrc.utexas.edu/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Austin\, Texas\, USA. On-site research in the Ransom Center's coll
 ections\; international applicants welcome and receive an additional one-t
 ime USD 500 stipend for visa and travel costs.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-philosophy-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T151759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Philosophy Competition: First Edition
DESCRIPTION:The AI Philosophy Competition\, organised by philosophers Zacha
 ry Goodsell and Elliott Thornley and funded by Forethought Research\, aims
  to gauge the philosophical capabilities of AI in a way transparent to the
  philosophical community.\n\nOrganisation: Zachary Goodsell and Elliott Th
 ornley\, funded by Forethought Research\nLocation: Online submission via O
 penReview (preferred) or Google Forms\; open internationally.\nAward: USD 
 3\,000 first prize\, USD 2\,000 second\, USD 1\,000 third\, plus a USD 5\,
 000 pool for creative methodology distributed at the judges' discretion (U
 SD 11\,000 total). No entry fee.\nDuration: Finalists announced early Dece
 mber 2026\; results announced end of January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.za
 charygoodsell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.zacharygoodsell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission via OpenReview (preferred) or Google Forms\; ope
 n internationally.
END:VEVENT
